{
  "name": "PassedOnUnread",
  "comment": "A format type keeps its raw bytes until first use (ruling 6): a json file read and\nwritten back on, unchanged, is byte-for-byte the same. config.json is spelled with\nunusual whitespace and keys out of order so any re-parse-and-write would reformat and\nthe file sizes would diverge. We compare the two files' sizes directly through the\npath type (a byte-level fact that doesn't go through the json parse). Sizes are read\ninto variables BEFORE the copy is deleted so the lazy size read sees the live file.\nRed today — today's write reformats config.json's 65 bytes to a 40-byte compact json\n({\"zebra\":1,\"alpha\":\"two\",\"beta\":[3,4,5]}).",
  "step": [
    {
      "index": 0,
      "text": "file.exists config.json, write to %origPath%",
      "line": {
        "number": 10
      },
